{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "תמול שלשום", :translit "temol shilshom", :gloss "yesterday and the day before"} {:he "אל עם אשר לא ידעת תמול שלשום", :translit "el am asher lo yadata temol shilshom", :gloss "unto a people you did not know yesterday and the day before"} {:he "אל עם אשר לא ידעה תמול שלשום", :translit "el am asher lo yada'ah temol shilshom", :gloss "unto a people she did not know yesterday and the day before"} {:he "אשר לא ידעום תמול שלשום", :translit "asher lo yeda'um temol shilshom", :gloss "who did not know them yesterday and the day before"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["תמול שלשום"]} {:kind :other, :note "Midrashic contrast between Ruth and Israel: both enter unfamiliar peoples marked by the phrase; from each emerges a redeemer."}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'temol shilshom' occurs exactly twice in Scripture: once here in Exodus 5:8 and once in Ruth 2:11 ('unto a people she did not know yesterday and the day before'). Ruth voluntarily came to an unfamiliar people and from her descended David, the prototype of the messianic redeemer (Ruth 4:17-22). Israel came to Egypt, an unfamiliar people, and were oppressed, enslaved, and had their children cast into the Nile (Exodus 1:11-22); from them emerged Moses, who struck Egypt with ten plagues, extracted Israel with their wealth, and drowned the Egyptians in the sea (Exodus 7-14).", :sources ["Exodus 5:8" "Ruth 2:11" "Ruth 4:17-22" "Exodus 1:11-22" "Exodus 7-14"]}} {:quotes [{:he "צועקים", :translit "tzo'akim", :gloss "crying out"} {:he "צועקים אלי מן האדמה", :translit "tzo'akim elai min ha-adamah", :gloss "crying out to me from the earth"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["צועקים"]} {:kind :other, :note "The two occurrences of 'tzo'akim' are linked: Pharaoh's oppression in Exodus evokes the language of Abel's blood crying from the ground."}], :frame {:text "The word 'tzo'akim' (crying out) occurs exactly twice in Scripture: here in Exodus 5:8 and in Genesis 4:10, where God says to Cain, 'The voice of your brother's blood cries out to me from the earth.' Cain spilled innocent blood, and Pharaoh decreed that Israel fill brick quotas by pressing their own bodies into the buildings, effectively killing them through labor. Just as Abel's blood cried from the earth, so Israel's suffering under Pharaoh's decree cries out.", :sources ["Exodus 5:8" "Genesis 4:10"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["תמול שלשום"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "תמול שלשום", :refs ["Ruth 2:11"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "Midrashic contrast between Ruth and Israel: both enter unfamiliar peoples marked by the phrase; from each emerges a redeemer.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["צועקים"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "צועקים", :refs ["Genesis 4:10" "Exodus 5:8"], :n 2, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :other, :note "The two occurrences of 'tzo'akim' are linked: Pharaoh's oppression in Exodus evokes the language of Abel's blood crying from the ground.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:13:51.644723216Z", :model "glm-5.2"}